  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nThe main concern today is for critical fire weather conditions.\nHigh temperatures will range from near 80 over the west to near 90\nover the east. Minimum daytime humidities are expected to be in\nthe teens to low 20s. Breezy winds out of the southwest will be\naround 30 to 50 mph over the mountains and foothills, with gusts\ninto the 20s mph extending out into the plains. As a result, a Red\nFlag Warning is in effect from noon to 9 PM MDT today for western\nand central areas. Currently, conditions look similar for Friday\nand fire weather is expected to remain elevated into the end of\nthe work week.\n\nA low to the west continues to keep upper level flow out of the\nsouthwest and a weak shortwave or two will bring a low chance \n(10-30%) for showers and thunderstorms, primarily to the \nmountains, through Saturday. A high set up southeast of the region\nwill continue to allow hot temperatures over the east. Friday\nlooks to be the warmest day, with highs in the upper 90s near the\nDakota border.\n\nSunday into early next week, the low to the west is expected to\nmove into the region, bringing a more widespread chance (30-60%)\nfor showers and thunderstorms late Sunday through Monday. With\nthe approach of this low, atmospheric moisture will increase over\nthe region with PWATs around 160-200% of normal. This will bring\npotential for pockets of heavy rain with any storms. Cooler\ntemperatures will also move in, with highs in the low 70s over the\nwest to low 80s over the east by Monday.\n\nInto the middle of next week, the pattern looks to remain\nunsettled. This would allow for temperatures to remain near to\nslightly below normal (70s) and a low chance (10-40%) for daily\nshowers and thunderstorms. Archer",
